    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2204.13103_rag::chunk_018</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph neural networks (GNNs) are gaining popularity as a promising approach to machine learning on graphs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs extend traditional graph processing with the endto-end learning capability of deep learning</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">a wide range of GNNs, with great flexibility to support emerging GNNs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">apply neural architectures to perform message passing over graph structures</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph neural networks (GNN) analysis engines are vital for real-world problems that use large graph models.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In a GNN, partitioning a graph (as in [16]) yields several smaller sub-graphs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs exhibit a much more diverse mix of primitive operations than traditional graph processing and CNNs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Recent advances in graph neural networks (GNNs) have addressed this challenge with considerable success</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph neural networks (GNNs) have recently exploded in popularity thanks to their broad applicability to ubiquitous graph-related problems</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Recently developed graph sampling approaches (e.g., DGL [ 1 ], GraphSAGE [ 18 ], PinSAGE [ 49 ], and FastGCN [ 9 ]) alleviate scalability challenges of GNNs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Pruning methods have been proposed for accelerating GNN inference</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Various GNNs (Kipf &amp; Welling, 2017; Velickovic et al., 2018; Xu et al., 2018; Li et al., 2019) mainly differ in the employed aggregation functions.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs exhibit much higher memory usage than DNNs and traditional graph processing.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs inherit both the irregular processing dataow of graph analytic and the regular computing pattern of DNNs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs exhibit a much more diverse mix of primitive operations than traditional graph processing</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GenGNN , the first generic and flexible FPGA accelerator framework for a wide range of GNNs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We proposed FlowGNN , the rst generic and exible accelerator framework for a wide range of GNNs.</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/S0/R1#cuda_stream">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/S0/R1#entity"/>
    <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CUDA Stream</rdfs:label>
    <skos:skos_definition rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A sequence of operations that execute in order on the GPU, used for non-blocking data transfers and synchronization.</skos:skos_definition>
    <hasCQ rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CQ_S0_R1_0094</hasCQ>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CQ_S0_R1_0094</prov:wasDerivedFrom>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2106.05609_rag::chunk_011</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Synchronization is done by synchronizing the respective CUDA stream before inputting the transferred data into the GNN layer.</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
